    "name": "Hua Xin",
    "state_name": "Hua",
    "adjective": "Hua Xin",
    "mode": "standard",
    "leader_template": "3k_main_template_historical_hua_xin_hero_water",
    "subtitle": "Ignoble Opportunist",
    "description": "Renowned as a scholar, a bureaucrat and as a civil official, Hua Xin has always believed that positions of authority should be given to those with the right talents and correct abilities, rather than through birth-right or bribery. Fearing that his ideals would endanger him with Dong Zhuo's tyrannical rise, he was able to persuade the despot to allow him to govern some far off southern territory instead, and used this chance to flee from the capital. Along the way he briefly encountered the army of Yuan Shu, and advised him to take an immediate and aggressive stance against the usurper Dong. With the esteemed Yuan hesitant to commit, Hua Xin continued on his journey south. Now, still considered a loyal official of the Han, he has begun setting up his own local government and administration. He begins promoting only men of merit and capability, and making political reforms in the regional bureaucracy. Those closest to him will know how privately critical Hua Xin is of the Han court, and how he desires more drastic and sweeping changes to the entire system of governance. For now, at least, all he can do is play his role as a minor and loyal official, far from the capital, at least until an opportunity for reformation presents itself.",
